celeritas.options.render_distance.tooltip=The render distance controls how far away terrain will be rendered. Shorter distances mean that less terrain will be rendered improving frame rates.
celeritas.options.brightness.tooltip=Controls the brightness (gamma) of the game.
celeritas.options.gui_scale.tooltip=Sets the maximum scale factor to be used for the user interface. If 'auto' is used then the largest scale factor will always be used.
celeritas.options.fullscreen.tooltip=If enabled the game will display in full-screen (if supported).
celeritas.options.vsync.tooltip=If enabled the game's frame rate will be synchronized to the monitor's refresh rate making for a generally smoother experience at the expense of overall input latency. This setting might reduce performance if your system is too slow.
celeritas.options.framerate_limit.tooltip=Limits the maximum number of frames per second. This can help reduce battery usage and general system load when multi-tasking. If VSync is enabled this option will be ignored unless it is lower than your display's refresh rate.
celeritas.options.view_bobbing.tooltip=If enabled the player's view will sway and bob when moving around. Players who experience motion sickness while playing can benefit from disabling this.
celeritas.options.graphics.tooltip=The default graphics quality controls some legacy options and is necessary for mod compatibility. If the options below are left to Default they will use this setting.
celeritas.options.anaglyph.tooltip=Renders red/cyan stereoscopic output.
celeritas.options.clouds.tooltip=Controls the presence of rendered clouds in the sky.
celeritas.options.particles.tooltip=Controls the maximum number of particles which can be present on screen at any one time.
celeritas.options.smooth_lighting.tooltip=Controls whether blocks will be smoothly lit and shaded. This slightly increases the amount of time needed to re-build a chunk but doesn't affect frame rates.
celeritas.options.biome_blend.name=Biome Blend
celeritas.options.biome_blend.tooltip=Controls the range sampled when blending biome colors. Higher values produce smoother transitions.
celeritas.options.mipmap_levels.tooltip=Controls the number of mipmaps which will be used for block model textures. Higher values provide better rendering of blocks in the distance but may adversely affect performance with many animated textures.
celeritas.options.chunk_builder_threads.name=Chunk Builder Threads
celeritas.options.chunk_builder_threads.tooltip=Specifies the number of threads to use for chunk building. Using more threads can speed up chunk loading and update speed but may negatively impact frame times.
celeritas.options.defer_chunk_updates.name=Always Defer Chunk Updates
celeritas.options.defer_chunk_updates.tooltip=If enabled rendering will never wait for chunk updates to finish even if they are important. This can greatly improve frame rates in some scenarios but it may create significant visual lag in the world.
celeritas.options.async_occlusion.name=Async Occlusion Culling
celeritas.options.async_occlusion.tooltip=Performs occlusion/frustum culling asynchronously, up to one frame behind the current camera position. This can improve frametimes, particularly at higher render distances, but at the cost of chunks popping into view if the camera is turned too aggressively.
celeritas.options.fog_culling.name=Fog Culling
celeritas.options.fog_culling.tooltip=If enabled chunks which are determined to be fully hidden by fog effects will not be rendered helping to improve performance. The improvement can be more dramatic when fog effects are heavier (such as while underwater) but it may cause undesirable visual artifacts between the sky and fog in some scenarios.
celeritas.options.entity_culling.name=Entity Culling
celeritas.options.entity_culling.tooltip=If enabled entities determined not to be in any visible chunks will be skipped during rendering. This can help improve performance by avoiding the rendering of entities located underground or behind walls.
celeritas.options.entity_occlusion_interval.name=Entity Occlusion Interval
celeritas.options.entity_occlusion_interval.tooltip=Minimum delay between occlusion queries for an entity.
celeritas.options.particle_culling.name=Particle Culling
celeritas.options.particle_culling.tooltip=Skips particles outside the camera frustum.
celeritas.options.entity_instancing.name=Entity Instancing
celeritas.options.entity_instancing.tooltip=Batches compatible entity geometry into instanced draw calls.
celeritas.options.animate_visible_textures.name=Animate Only Visible Textures
celeritas.options.animate_visible_textures.tooltip=If enabled only animated textures determined to be visible will be updated. This can provide a significant boost to frame rates on some hardware especially with heavier resource packs. If you experience issues with some textures not being animated try disabling this option.
celeritas.options.translucency_sorting.name=Translucent Face Sorting
celeritas.options.translucency_sorting.tooltip=If enabled translucent effects from surfaces such as stained glass and water will be applied correctly.
celeritas.options.safe_chunk_edges.name=Safe Chunk Edges
celeritas.options.safe_chunk_edges.tooltip=Waits for neighboring chunks before building edge geometry.
celeritas.options.compact_vertex_format.name=Use Compact Vertex Format
celeritas.options.compact_vertex_format.tooltip=If enabled a more compact vertex format will be used for rendering chunks. This can reduce graphics memory usage and bandwidth requirements significantly especially for integrated graphics cards but can cause z-fighting with some resource packs due to how it reduces the precision of position and texture coordinate attributes.
celeritas.options.check_gl_errors.name=OpenGL Error Checks
celeritas.options.check_gl_errors.tooltip=Checks for OpenGL errors during the game loop. Useful for debugging, but slower.
